# modules.cpp platform MCU provider specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented pure C++ platform-provider mechanism
for mm.mcu in release v1.2.0. status: official gives it the same authority as
the other implemented specifications in docs, and it supersedes every earlier
proposal of the same mechanism.

docs/modules-mm.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented manifest schema.
docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains authoritative for SDK and board selection,
responsibilities, and link ownership. docs/modules-build.mdy remains
authoritative for the implemented dependency closure and link pipeline.
docs/modules-c++20.mdy remains authoritative for permitted project code.
docs/modules-stdio.mdy owns the separate platform console role; USB CDC and
semihosting are not UART instances and do not change mm.mcu's UART contract.

# Decision

platform-interface and platform-provider belong to source-manifest version mm:
1.2 and are part of release v1.2.0. An older binary rejects manifests using
them rather than assigning them an older meaning.

The portable interface remains the globally unique core module mm.mcu. Each
platform implementation is a different, globally unique provider module. A
selected SDK or board binds the interface to one provider. The build injects
that provider into an executable only when the executable's ordinary dependency
closure reaches mm.mcu.

An application declares only:

Opening fence
use: mm.mcu
Closing fence

It never names a board, SDK, vendor library, or provider module. Platform
selection supplies the provider exactly as it already supplies startup code,
runtime support, and link ownership.

The mechanism does not relax global module-name uniqueness. It does not make a
board source part of the module dependency graph. It does not expose a generic
mm_mcu C ABI as the portable interface.

# Required invariants

- mm.mcu has one meaning in the whole repository.
- Every provider module has a globally unique module name.
- For each interface it binds, one selected platform resolves exactly one
  effective provider, with a selected board overriding its selected SDK's
  default. A target executable requiring an unbound interface is unavailable.
- An application neither names nor imports its provider.
- A provider is linked only when the executable closure requires its interface.
- An executable that does not use mm.mcu receives no mm.mcu provider object.
- A missing, duplicate, or target-unavailable provider is diagnosed before
  linking.
- Project and external CMake link paths receive the same augmented object list.
- The model exposes the binding without collapsing duplicate module names.
- Core applications remain core because the provider is supplied by the
  selected platform, not declared by the application.

# Why duplicate provider names are rejected

Several platforms must not export interchangeable modules all named lib.mcu.
The project loader rejects duplicate module names before lane filtering. That
invariant prevents index_of_module from selecting whichever declaration happens
to appear first in manifest walk order.

Weakening the check would make correctness depend on every availability rule
leaving exactly one declaration alive. If two survived, build and test could
silently select different implementations after an innocent folder reorder.
Model and document also load the unfiltered project and could expose an
arbitrary provider. A platform role therefore does not redefine module
identity.

Provider names identify implementations rather than roles. Examples are:

- platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
- platform.pico.mcu
- platform.mps2_an385.mcu

These names are explicit module identifiers, not values derived mechanically
from platform names. C++ module components are identifiers, so a platform name
containing a hyphen commonly uses an underscore in its provider module name:
rp2040-ram may bind platform.rp2040_ram.mcu. The platform-provider declaration
is the authoritative mapping; no tool infers it from either spelling.

One provider may be bound by several compatible boards or SDKs. Two different
providers may implement the same interface because their module names remain
different.

# Why board-source dependencies are insufficient

Board file entries are compiled as one synthetic board unit and their objects
are appended to every executable in the lane. They have no use declarations and
do not participate in order or closure traversal.

Allowing board sources to import modules would be a useful independent feature,
but it would not provide conditional implementation selection. A board source
that registers a Platform subclass refers to mm.mcu. Appending that object to an
executable whose closure does not contain mm.mcu creates undefined references.
Appending mm.mcu and the provider to every executable instead makes unused
applications pay for the interface.

The observed module-attached vtable and set_platform link failures demonstrate
that mismatch. They do not establish that a module-attached polymorphic class is
unusable. When an application reaches mm.mcu and the build also injects the
selected provider closure, both sides of those references are present.

# Manifest declarations

Two mm: 1.2 declarations express the relationship. docs/modules-mm.mdy lists
them in the closed key table and the shared manifest gate enforces their version
and kind placement in every manifest-reading tool.

## platform-interface

platform-interface is an optional marker valid only on kind: module. It occurs
at most once and must have no value. Its presence marks the manifest's module as
a portable interface that requires a selected platform provider at executable
link time. The module declaration already supplies the interface identity, so
the marker does not repeat that name or create a mismatch case.

The mm.mcu manifest declares:

Opening fence
mm: 1.2
kind: module
name: mcu
module: mm.mcu
platform-interface:
file: status.cppm mm.mcu:status
file: board.cppm mm.mcu:board
file: spi-types.cppm mm.mcu:spi_types
file: i2c-types.cppm mm.mcu:i2c_types
file: adc-types.cppm mm.mcu:adc_types
file: pwm-types.cppm mm.mcu:pwm_types
file: platform.cppm mm.mcu:platform
file: gpio.cppm mm.mcu:gpio
file: spi.cppm mm.mcu:spi
file: i2c.cppm mm.mcu:i2c
file: uart.cppm mm.mcu:uart
file: timer.cppm mm.mcu:timer
file: adc.cppm mm.mcu:adc
file: pwm.cppm mm.mcu:pwm
file: mcu.cppm mm.mcu
file: src/platform.cpp
file: src/analog.cpp
Closing fence

Source order is declared, and the order carries a fact: the type partitions
precede the platform partition, whose description methods return their types,
and the call partitions follow it, because they call through it.

The declaration does not make the module itself unavailable. mm.mcu can be
compiled, checked, modelled, and documented without selecting a platform. The
provider becomes a requirement only when linking an executable whose closure
contains the interface.

## platform-provider

platform-provider is repeated and valid on kind: sdk and kind: board. Each value
contains an interface module name followed by one provider module name:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
Closing fence

Both names are required. Extra fields are rejected. The interface must resolve
to a module carrying the platform-interface marker. The provider must resolve
to a different kind: module target, and its use closure must reach the interface
module.

All declarations are resolved after the complete manifest tree is collected,
so folder order is irrelevant. Referencing an unknown interface or provider is a
manifest error naming the declaration and available candidates.

# Provider ownership and selection

The effective platform is the selected SDK plus its optional selected board.
For each required interface, the selected board binding is more specific than
the selected SDK binding.

- A board owns a provider implemented by project-linked board support.
- An SDK owns a default provider supplied by its runtime or external platform
  library.
- A selected board binding for an interface overrides the selected SDK's
  binding for that same interface.
- More than one binding for the same interface in one SDK or one board is a
  manifest error.
- A binding on an unselected SDK or board has no effect on the lane.
- A provider named by several compatible platform definitions remains one
  module with one identity.

Board override is deterministic structural precedence, not declaration order.
It supports the ordinary platform pattern in which an SDK supplies a general
implementation while one board specializes pin multiplexing, UART selection,
clocking, or another facility. Requiring the SDK to withdraw its default for
each specialized board would move board knowledge into the SDK declaration.
Unlike first-match or walk-order selection, most-specific-wins cannot silently
change after a folder reorder.

The current platforms declare:

Opening fence
# platforms/pico/rp2040-ram/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.rp2040_ram.mcu

# platforms/pico/sdk/pico-arm/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.pico.mcu

# platforms/pico/sdk/pico-riscv/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.pico.mcu
Closing fence

rp2350-ram and mps2-an385 declare no binding until they have real, tested
implementations. A portable application requiring mm.mcu is unavailable in
those lanes rather than failing late with undefined symbols.

# Provider modules

A provider is an ordinary kind: module with a globally unique name and a use
edge to its interface. Appearing as the right-hand side of any platform-provider
declaration classifies it as a provider target.

A provider target is not an independent root. It is available only when the
selected platform binds its interface to that provider, and it enters an
executable closure only through provider injection. This prevents a root build
from compiling every platform implementation and prevents an unrelated
application from receiving provider static initializers.

The direct-register RP2040 provider has this shape:

Opening fence
mm: 1.1
kind: module
name: rp2040-ram-mcu
module: platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
use: mm.mcu
file: mcu.cppm
option: buildable-host no
Closing fence

It imports mm.mcu, derives from mm::mcu::Platform, implements the facilities the
board supports, and registers one object during static initialization. It uses
the RP2040 register map directly and names no third-party library.

The Pico SDK provider has this shape:

Opening fence
mm: 1.1
kind: module
name: pico-mcu
module: platform.pico.mcu
use: mm.mcu
file: mcu.cppm
option: buildable-host no
Closing fence

It is project-owned platform code, not the public wrapper for a vendored
library. It therefore does not use the lib. module-name prefix and does not
declare library: pico-sdk. Its availability comes from the selected SDK's
platform-provider binding. Beneath its pure C++ interface it may call a private
project-owned C adapter ABI implemented by the Pico external bridge. That ABI is
specific to the bridge and is not the portable mm.mcu contract.

# Pure C++ interface

mm.mcu exports enum class types and a Platform base class. Unsupported remains a
first-class result, so one provider may implement GPIO while inheriting honest
Unsupported answers for SPI, I2C, UART, time, ADC, and PWM.

The platform partition owns:

- Platform with virtual facility operations and Unsupported defaults;
- a virtual board description supplied on demand by the registered provider,
  and the two analog inventories, answered the same way;
- set_platform taking a Platform reference;
- platform returning the registered object or an unserved fallback.

The implementation unit owns the fallback object, the current pointer,
set_platform, and platform; a second implementation unit, src/analog.cpp, owns
the analog facilities' arithmetic and lookups. Public facility functions call
platform and return typed Status values. An application sees no C
declarations, integer status codes, adapter headers, or vendor types.

The provider owns its subclass and registration object. The provider object is
linked directly, not extracted from an archive, so its namespace-scope
registration initializer is retained. The existing m68k external-link fixture
already proves that project object initializers survive the CMake hand-off.

## GPIO edge latch

This facility is the one addition model 2 of docs/modules-execution.mdy
makes to the blocking execution model: an interrupt sets a flag, and the
program asks for it. That document owns the rules the four calls below are
instances of.

`Edge` names Rising, Falling, or Both physical transitions, as `gpio_read`
reports level. `gpio_watch(pin, pull, edge)` takes over an existing GPIO
configuration or configures an input, and starts with an empty latch. One
watch owns a pin. A second watch or `gpio_configure` on it answers Busy.
`gpio_unwatch` releases the pin and leaves it unconfigured.

`gpio_take(pin, pending)` is nonblocking. On Ok it reports and consumes at
least one selected edge observed but not yet reported. Edges coalesce: there
is no count, order, timestamp, or debounce. A queue-backed provider may
deliver an older edge on a later call. Calling take until false empties
delivered events, but does not establish a delivery barrier; use a new watch
for a known-empty start. An edge arriving during a take is delivered by that
call or the next. `gpio_wait(pin, timeout_ms, pending)` has the same result
but waits until an edge or the deadline; zero timeout is a take. Unrelated
wakeups and interrupted sleeps do not shorten the wait.

Take, wait, or unwatch without a watch answers BadArgument; an unavailable pin
or edge facility answers Unsupported. On failure, pending is unchanged and
watch leaves no partial ownership. A caller needing the current level reads
it separately with `gpio_read`. All four calls run from ordinary application
code, on the watching core where the provider requires that.

## Board description and future devices

The board partition exports three value types. Gpio carries the unsigned number
accepted by the GPIO facility and a stable, human-readable name. Led carries a
name, the number of the GPIO to which it is electrically attached, and whether
that signal is active high. Board carries the board name, a read-only
std::span<const Gpio> that enumerates every GPIO the board exposes exactly once,
and an optional Led. When an LED is present, its GPIO number must identify one
entry in that sequence.

board() returns the registered provider's description by value, while its span
views a provider-owned static GPIO table. A std::vector was tested first but its
heap allocation pulled newlib entropy support into the custom RP2040 RAM image;
the non-owning span retains vector-like size, indexing, and iteration without
allocating during provider registration, board discovery, or early bare-metal
startup. The unserved platform returns an empty name, empty GPIO span, and no
LED.

This is intentionally a board description rather than another platform-selection
mechanism. Board carries what is one fact for the whole board: its name, its
GPIOs, its LED. An inventory whose entries each carry facts of their own -- an
ADC channel with its reference and width, a PWM output with its counter and
period limits -- is answered by a Platform description method instead, so
that a per-entry fact never has to be promoted to a board-wide one that would
be wrong for some entry. Both kinds record the GPIOs they attach to by number;
facility operations remain on Platform, and applications continue importing
only mm.mcu. Adding a device class therefore grows a description without
creating provider module names in portable application manifests.

The current direct RP2040 provider describes rp2040-ram, GPIOs GP0 through GP29,
and an active-high LED on GP25. The Pico bridge reports the selected PICO_BOARD,
NUM_BANK0_GPIOS, and, when the SDK board declares one, its LED pin and polarity
to platform.pico.mcu; the provider turns those bridge-private values into the
same pure C++ Board representation. Pico W and Pico 2 W therefore have no
GPIO-backed LED in Board: their onboard LED belongs to the CYW43. The same
provider supplies GPIO, both hardware SPI instances, both hardware I2C
instances, the selected board's default hardware UART, and the monotonic timer
for every Pico SDK board declaration. Each hardware facility the adapter reaches
is its own Pico SDK library: the bridge names hardware_spi, hardware_i2c, and
hardware_uart explicitly, because pico_stdlib carries none of them.

## Bus facilities

SPI and I2C are the two bus facilities, and they differ in exactly one way that
reaches the interface: how a device is selected.

SPI configuration carries the instance, the clock and transmit GPIOs, an
optional receive GPIO, baud, mode, and bit order. It does not carry chip select.
Chip select belongs to the device transaction along with any data/command,
reset, and busy GPIOs, so a driver owns those pins and the facility never
changes them behind it.

I2C configuration carries the instance, the data and clock GPIOs, and baud,
which defaults to 400 kHz because that is the bus's common speed and a caller
that says nothing about it should still get a working bus. There is no mode or
bit order: the bus fixes both. There is no chip select either, because the
address is the selection, which is why every I2C operation takes an address and
the configuration does not.

Addresses are seven-bit. Ten-bit addressing is a real feature of the bus that no
device this interface has met uses, and inventing an encoding for it before one
appears would be guessing at the shape of the answer.

i2c_write_read is one operation rather than a write followed by a read. A
register read is a write of the register number and then a read, and the bus
must not be released between them: another master may take it, and a device that
tracks its own address pointer would answer from the wrong place. Offering the
two halves separately would leave that gap open for a later refactor to walk
through, so the interface does not offer them.

A provider that implements the operation must hold the bus across it. The Pico
provider does so through the SDK's nostop argument, which makes the read a
repeated start rather than a second transaction.

## Analog facilities

ADC and PWM are the two analog facilities, partitions beside SPI and I2C with
inventories the platform answers per channel and per output.

Opening fence
struct AdcChannel {
    unsigned int number;                 // what the ADC functions accept
    std::string_view name;
    std::optional<unsigned int> gpio;    // absent for an internal source
    unsigned int bits;                   // a count lies in [0, 2^bits)
    unsigned int reference_millivolts;   // full scale, or zero if unknown
};
struct AdcDescription { std::span<const AdcChannel> channels; };

struct PwmOutput {
    unsigned int number;                 // what the PWM functions accept
    std::string_view name;
    std::optional<unsigned int> gpio;
    unsigned int group;                  // the counter it shares a period with
    unsigned int comparator;             // its compare register in that counter
    std::uint64_t minimum_period_ns;     // both zero when the provider cannot
    std::uint64_t maximum_period_ns;     // advertise them
};
struct PwmDescription { std::span<const PwmOutput> outputs; };

[[nodiscard]] AdcDescription adc_description();
[[nodiscard]] Status adc_configure(unsigned int channel);
[[nodiscard]] Status adc_read(unsigned int channel, unsigned int& count);
[[nodiscard]] Status adc_release(unsigned int channel);
[[nodiscard]] Status adc_channel_for_gpio(unsigned int gpio,
                                          unsigned int& channel);
[[nodiscard]] unsigned int adc_millivolts(const AdcChannel&,
                                           unsigned int count);

[[nodiscard]] PwmDescription pwm_description();
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_configure(unsigned int output,
                                   std::uint64_t period_ns);
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_period(unsigned int output, std::uint64_t& actual_ns);
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_write(unsigned int output, std::uint64_t duty_ns);
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_release(unsigned int output);
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_output_for_gpio(unsigned int gpio,
                                         unsigned int& output);
[[nodiscard]] Status pwm_plan(const PwmCounter&, std::uint64_t period_ns,
                              PwmPlan&);
Closing fence

Each facility addresses its own inventory by number. An entry names the GPIO
it is attached to when it is attached to one -- a temperature sensor is a
channel with none -- and the two lookups find the number for a pin, so a layer
that speaks in pins, a sketch's analogRead say, is one lookup and one call. A
number outside the inventory is BadArgument; a number the inventory carries
but the platform does not implement is Unsupported. The spans view
provider-owned static storage, as Board::gpios does, and every output
parameter changes only on Ok.

adc_configure claims a channel: a pin channel's pad goes to the converter and
an internal source is enabled; on a channel already claimed it is idempotent.
adc_read performs one bounded conversion and answers a count in [0, 2^bits);
on an unconfigured channel it is BadArgument. adc_release is idempotent,
leaves a pin unconfigured, and disables an internal source. One-shot reading
is the only timing mode. A channel's bits lie in [1, 31]; its reference is
the full-scale voltage when the board knows it and zero when it does not,
which does not prevent a raw read. adc_millivolts is count times reference
over 2^bits - 1, rounded to nearest in 64-bit integer arithmetic, and zero
for a zero reference, bits outside the range, or a count past full scale.
Temperature conversion is not here: an internal temperature channel has no
universal formula.

pwm_configure claims an output, sets the nearest period the hardware holds,
and starts it at zero duty; pwm_period answers that actual period, which is
what a duty is measured against, because an approximated 490 Hz is not
2,040,816 ns. A zero period is BadArgument, and so is one outside nonzero
advertised limits, before any hardware changes; zero limits mean unknown, not
unlimited, and the provider may still refuse. Outputs in one group share a
counter: a second may join only at the group's requested period, and another
request is Busy. Outputs with one group and one comparator are aliases of one
compare register, and a second claim is Busy even at the same period. The
same output at the same requested period is idempotent; at another it is
Busy until released. pwm_write accepts a duty from zero through the actual
period -- zero a steady low, the period a steady high -- and refuses more
with BadArgument; a change takes effect no later than the next complete
period without a runt pulse. pwm_release is idempotent, leaves the pin
unconfigured, does not disturb a sibling, and stops the counter after the
group's last output goes.

pwm_plan is arithmetic for a counter whose period is clock ticks times a
fixed-point divider times top + 1, with the compare register the counter's
width -- the RP2040's and RP2350's shape. It takes the smallest divider at
which the period fits, so the top is as large as it can be, and the nearest
top; a divider fits when the period in counts at that divider, rounded to
nearest with a half rounding up, is at most 2^counter_bits - 1. The top is
therefore never more than 2^counter_bits - 2, so that top + 1, the steady
high, fits the register: full duty is the ordinary path on every part. The
cost is one count, except in the half-count interval at each divider's limit
where a period that would have rounded to 2^counter_bits counts takes the
next divider step and loses that step's fraction of its duty steps; at 125
MHz and divider one that interval is 524,284 through 524,288 ns. It answers
BadArgument for a zero clock or one past 2^32, a counter of fewer than two
or more than thirty-two bits, a divider of more than twenty-four bits, a
period under two ticks or beyond the counter's reach, or an intermediate
that would overflow, and it commits its plan only on Ok. It uses no 128-bit
integer. A counter of another shape plans for itself.

Polarity, phase-correct counting, capture, DMA-fed sequences, servo, and
tone are outside these facilities; a tone belongs above PWM.

## Pad ownership

The selected platform owns GPIO, ADC, and PWM state together, and a pad is
one thing at a time. A plain GPIO configuration is a replaceable claim:
adc_configure or pwm_configure may take it over, and on success the digital
mode -- and, for an output, its level -- is gone. A watched GPIO answers
Busy, and its latch is untouched. An ADC or PWM claim blocks gpio_configure,
gpio_watch, gpio_write, gpio_read, and a competing analog claim with Busy;
a refused gpio_read leaves its output unchanged. Release returns the pad to
an unconfigured state.

The asymmetry is deliberate. A digital configuration is a mode and a level,
and a caller that wants them back issues gpio_configure and then gpio_write,
two calls between which the pad passes through the mux change and the
configured default level, so a transition can appear on the wire: a
consequence the caller can foresee and sequence. An analog claim carries
running hardware -- a counter with a period and a duty, a converter with a
selected input -- and a group another output may share, so replacing it
silently would stop a waveform or move a sibling's period without the owner
hearing. Digital therefore yields to analog and analog yields to nothing but
release; a layer that stops a waveform by driving the pin, as Arduino's
digitalWrite does after analogWrite, releases first.

A takeover is transactional where hardware permits: the provider validates
inventory, group, range, and ownership first, changes the mux and peripheral,
and records the new owner last, so a failed call leaves its record true. If
hardware cannot be restored, the provider records the pin as unconfigured and
answers TransportError rather than claim a configuration it cannot verify.
This is not a universal pin arbiter: SPI and I2C configuration registers no
pad claim, and board wiring must not assign those signals to an analog pin at
the same time.

# Per-executable closure augmentation

Provider requirements are computed before the lane's filtered target tree is
constructed. A shared post-load analysis operates on the complete
project.targets collection and its globally resolved use names:

1. Validate global module identity and the authored use graph.
2. Compute each application and test's transitive platform-interface
   requirements over that unfiltered graph.
3. Resolve the selected SDK defaults and selected board overrides.
4. Record the effective provider, or the reason no provider can be selected,
   for each required interface.
5. Follow each selected provider's authored closure and add any further
   platform interfaces it reaches, repeating to a fixed point.
6. Derive node availability and provider compilation requirements from that
   complete record.
7. Only then construct the filtered build or test tree.

This ordering avoids asking an already filtered tree to discover the dependency
that determines whether a node survives filtering. Build and test use this same
analysis result even though their current root-selection filters differ. The
provider mechanism must not be implemented as separate approximations in
tools/build and tools/test.

The build computes one augmented closure for each executable or test.

1. Compute the ordinary use closure from the requested application or test.
2. Collect every module in that closure declaring platform-interface.
3. Resolve each interface through the selected SDK and board bindings.
4. Validate that the selected provider's structural capability is available in
   the lane; a provider filtered by buildable-target or buildable-host cannot
   leave an executable linked silently against the interface fallback.
5. Compute the provider's ordinary dependency closure.
6. Repeat from step 2 for interfaces reached only through that provider, until
   no new provider is found.
7. Merge the application and provider closures without repeating targets or
   object paths, preserving the existing authored-closure object order.
8. Append the selected board unit as today.
9. Hand the same final ordered object list to project link or external_link.

Provider closure traversal uses the existing cycle and unknown-module
diagnostics. Fixed-point expansion is independent of manifest order: an
interface reached from a provider is resolved even when its target appeared
earlier in the walk. A provider whose closure does not reach its declared
interface is rejected during project validation rather than linked as an
unrelated object.

Compilation planning must include the selected providers required by any
retained executable. Non-selected providers remain unavailable skipped targets.
A provider is compiled once even when several applications require it, while
its object is added only to each requiring application's link closure.

# Availability behavior

Platform-interface requirements apply at target executable selection, not when
merely loading or compiling the interface module. A host lane binds no target
platform provider and links the interface fallback, allowing a host unit test
to register a stand-in Platform. Applications such as mcu-blink that have no
useful host behavior remain explicitly buildable-host no.

For a target root build, an application whose closure requires an interface
with no selected provider is unavailable and is counted among skipped targets.
Other applications in the lane continue to build. An explicitly requested
target application or test exits with the existing unavailable-target status
and a diagnostic such as:

build: app mcu-blink requires platform interface mm.mcu, but board rp2350-ram and SDK arm-none-eabi-newlib provide no binding

A repeated binding within one SDK or board is a manifest error. A binding on
both the selected SDK and selected board is not ambiguous: the board binding is
the effective one and the SDK binding remains its default.

An application not reaching mm.mcu has no provider requirement even when the
selected platform offers one. target-smoke-any therefore receives no MCU
provider object.

# Core boundary

Core describes the dependencies authored by project code; it is not a promise
that every object in the final platform executable is core. A core executable
may contain non-core runtime or provider objects supplied by its selected
platform. This is already true when a selected SDK supplies newlib, glibc, or an
external platform library without the application declaring that dependency.

Provider injection is likewise a platform edge, not a use declaration authored
by the application. A core application remains core because its authored graph
still cannot declare a third-party library or use an arbitrary non-core module.
For example, platform.pico.mcu may be non-core because it delegates to the Pico
SDK adapter while the portable application importing only core mm.mcu remains
core.

The exemption is narrow:

- the provider must be the exact module named by the selected SDK or board;
- it is injected only for the exact interface named by that binding;
- the provider must depend on that interface;
- it creates no general permission for a core node to use non-core modules;
- a module declaring library remains a lib. wrapper and follows the existing
  core prohibition.

Structural-property validation must evaluate the selected platform edge with
this explicit rule rather than suppressing core checks generally.

# Model

The abstract model exposes the mechanism without selecting arbitrary duplicate
modules.

- ModuleNode exposes whether it carries the platform-interface marker; its
  existing module identity is the interface identity.
- SdkNode and BoardNode expose their platform-provider bindings.
- Platform exposes the effective bindings after SDK and board selection.
- A workflow or artifact query can derive an application's selected provider
  closure from these bindings without changing its authored use closure.

Authored imports remain authored facts. Provider injection is reported as a
platform binding, not inserted into ModuleNode::uses, so model clients can tell
what the application declared from what the lane supplied.

# External CMake bridge

External linking receives the augmented project object list unchanged from both
build and test. The Pico provider object therefore reaches CMake only for
applications and target tests using mm.mcu. Its static initializer is preserved
by the existing EXTERNAL_OBJECT hand-off and direct object ordering.

The bridge continues to compile the private adapter that translates
project-owned C calls into Pico SDK calls. That adapter may remain lane-wide in
the first implementation. A later optimization may pass the required platform
interface set through mm-inputs.cmake and let the bridge omit facility adapters
for executables that do not need them. This optimization is not required for
correct provider selection.

No generic external-builder code names mm.mcu, a provider module, or a Pico
adapter. It transports the final object list and generic bridge inputs only.

# Implemented transition

mm.mcu exports its Platform base class, Board/Gpio/Led description, fallback
object, set_platform, and typed facility dispatch. platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
implements GPIO and board discovery directly. platform.pico.mcu implements the
same C++ interface and delegates beneath it to the bridge-private Pico adapter
ABI. The core interface contains no C ABI header, and the former lib.mcu
identity no longer exists.

mcu-blink imports only mm.mcu. Provider binding makes it available on
rp2040-ram and the Pico SDK lanes, and unavailable on target lanes with no
binding. Unrelated executables retain their ordinary closure.

# Conformance

- The complete project loads with globally unique module names.
- Two different platforms may bind mm.mcu to two different provider modules.
- Two bindings for mm.mcu in one SDK or one board fail before compilation and
  name their owner.
- A selected board binding for mm.mcu overrides a selected SDK default
  independently of manifest declaration and folder order.
- An unknown interface or provider fails at load and names its manifest.
- A provider whose use closure does not reach its interface is rejected.
- A selected provider unavailable for the target capability makes its requiring
  executable unavailable before the filtered target tree is constructed.
- An interface reached only through a selected provider is resolved to a fixed
  point; a missing nested provider makes the target unavailable before
  compilation, independently of manifest order.
- mcu-blink names only mm.mcu and remains core.
- mcu-blink discovers its LED GPIO and active level from Board rather than
  encoding either platform property.
- Every reported GPIO has a number and name; a reported LED names one GPIO in
  that inventory. The host stand-in covers an active-low LED, span iteration,
  and GPIO inventory boundaries.
- SPI configuration carries instance, signal GPIOs, baud, mode, and bit order;
  writes and full-duplex transfers preserve byte-span boundaries. Invalid
  instances, pins, and signal combinations are rejected.
- I2C configuration carries instance, data and clock GPIOs, and a baud that
  defaults to 400 kHz. An address no device answers, an unconfigured instance,
  and an empty transfer are each rejected, and a rejected call puts nothing on
  the bus.
- i2c_write_read reaches the provider as one operation, and a host stand-in
  observes one transaction rather than a write followed by a read.
- An unserved platform answers Unsupported for every I2C operation rather than
  failing to link.
- Both analog inventories reach the caller as the provider gave them, and the
  two lookups find an attached pin, refuse an unattached one, and leave their
  output alone when they refuse.
- Every ADC and PWM operation routes to the provider with its arguments,
  carries every status back, and leaves its output parameters unchanged on
  any answer but Ok; an unserved platform answers Unsupported for all of them.
- A plain GPIO yields to an analog claim and the GPIO facility answers Busy on
  that pad until release; a watched pad refuses the claim and keeps its watch;
  a takeover the hardware refuses leaves the GPIO claim recorded and drivable.
- A sibling at another period, an alias of a claimed comparator, and a duty
  past the period are refused; a sibling at the group's period joins; a
  release disturbs neither a sibling's duty nor its period.
- adc_millivolts is exact at zero, full scale, and the largest product the
  bounds allow, rounds to nearest, and answers zero for an unknown reference,
  bits outside the range, or a count past full scale.
- pwm_plan matches an independent rational model at both nominal Pico clocks
  for Arduino's 490 and 980 Hz, tone's 31 and 65,535 Hz, one kilohertz, and
  both ends of the range; at 125 MHz 524,280 and 524,281 ns plan at divider
  one and top 65,534 while 524,284, 524,286, and 524,288 ns plan at the next
  divider step; it refuses a zero period, one under two ticks, one beyond the
  counter's reach, a zero or over-wide clock, bit widths outside its bounds,
  and an overflowing scaling, leaving its output untouched each time.
- mcu-blink links on rp2040-ram through platform.rp2040_ram.mcu.
- With the corresponding external tools installed, mcu-blink links on Pico ARM
  and Pico RISC-V through platform.pico.mcu and the real external CMake bridge.
- analog-smoke builds for the RP2040 and both RP2350 architectures with the
  ADC and PWM adapter symbols present; its verdict on the wired fixture is a
  physical run.
- An explicit mcu-blink build on rp2350-ram or mps2-an385 reports the missing
  provider before compilation or linking.
- A root build on either unsupported board skips mcu-blink and still builds
  target-smoke-any successfully.
- target-smoke-any contains no provider-module registration object or
  provider-module symbols. A lane-wide external adapter is not mistaken for the
  conditionally injected provider module.
- The mm.mcu implementation object and selected provider appear exactly once in
  a requiring executable's ordered object list.
- The hosted external-link fixture executes a namespace-scope initializer from
  a directly handed-off project object, proving the retention mechanism on
  which provider registration relies.
- Project linking and external linking consume identical augmented closure
  semantics.
- The target-mcu-provider compile test receives the direct provider under
  rp2040-ram and delegates through the real external bridge under Pico SDK.
- Model output distinguishes authored use edges from selected provider bindings.
- Host build, all unit suites, check, model, and document pass in a configured
  development environment with the repository's documented host prerequisites.
- Release qualification additionally passes ARM and RISC-V Pico SDK integration
  when the required cross toolchains, Pico SDK checkout, and picotool package
  are available.

# Qualification environments

Unit fixtures for marker grammar, reference resolution, precedence,
availability, closure augmentation, and core validation are mandatory and do
not depend on Pico tools or hardware.

Real Pico ARM and Pico RISC-V links are environment-gated release qualification,
not reasons to weaken those fixtures or to report an implementation failure on
a machine lacking the tools. In this repository workspace the qualification is
currently exercisable: the Pico SDK bundle supplies its picotool package and
the riscv32-pico-elf tools through its bin directory. Other environments must
record a skipped qualification with the missing prerequisite named.

# What this does not settle

- It does not make arbitrary board sources import project modules.
- It does not permit duplicate module names per lane.
- It does not choose individual UART instances, GPIO pin ownership, or clocks.
- It does not promise that every provider supports every mm.mcu facility;
  Unsupported remains part of the interface, including SPI, I2C, ADC, and PWM.
  The Pico SDK provider implements both; the Linux implementation is planned,
  and every other provider inherits Unsupported for both.
- It does not make attached displays or sensors MCU facilities. SPI is an
  implemented reusable transport; mm.display and mm.epaper.ssd1680 are layered
  above it as specified by docs/modules-display.mdy and
  docs/modules-epaper.mdy. A physical display profile remains pending.
- It does not require a dynamically replaceable provider after program startup.
- It does not yet remove private C linkage at a vendor SDK boundary where the
  SDK itself is C. The portable application and provider contract remain pure
  C++ even when a provider delegates to such an adapter.

# Hosted Linux provider

platform.linux.mcu implements the same pure C++ Platform seam through Linux
kernel character devices. A provider method without initialize resolves its
device map on its first call. A syntactically invalid map makes board() empty
and operations return BadArgument; an invalid entry is confined to its instance.

Status::TransportError distinguishes a present transport that failed from
Unsupported, which means the facility or instance is absent. EACCES and EPERM
are transport failures, EBUSY is Busy, ETIMEDOUT is Timeout, an explicitly
named missing path is BadArgument, and an auto-discovered missing device is
Unsupported. A caller field rejected by a configuration ioctl is BadArgument;
an internal ioctl rejecting its own argument is TransportError. Fields a Linux
kernel API cannot apply are checked and rejected, never silently ignored.
